\details{
\strong{Local sensitivity.} The derivative of a posterior expectation with
respect to \code{log(alpha)} at \code{alpha = 1} equals a posterior covariance
(Giordano et al., 2018). For each parameter \eqn{\theta} with posterior
mean \eqn{\mu} and standard deviation \eqn{\sigma}, bmbeR reports
\itemize{
\item \code{prior_mean_sens} = \eqn{Cov(\theta, \log p(\theta)) / \sigma}: how many
posterior SDs the mean moves per unit change in \code{log(alpha)};
\item \code{prior_sd_sens} = \eqn{Cov((\theta-\mu)^2, \log p(\theta)) / (2\sigma^2)}:
the relative change in the posterior SD;
\item \code{lik_mean_sens}, \code{lik_sd_sens}: the same with the log-likelihood.
}

In a conjugate normal model with prior \eqn{N(m_0, s_0^2)} and likelihood
\eqn{N(\bar y, s^2)}, \code{-2 * prior_sd_sens} is the share of posterior
precision contributed by the prior, \code{-2 * lik_sd_sens} the share
contributed by the data, and
\code{conflict_z = prior_mean_sens / (2 * sqrt(prior_sd_sens * lik_sd_sens))}
equals \eqn{(m_0 - \bar y)/\sqrt{s_0^2 + s^2}}, the classic prior
predictive check of prior-data conflict (Box, 1980). Outside that model
these are approximations. See the "Prior sensitivity" article on the
package website for the derivations.

\strong{Diagnosis} (per parameter). The prior's \emph{influence} is
\verb{max(|prior_mean_sens|, |prior_sd_sens|)}: a prior matters if it moves the
posterior mean or adds precision. The likelihood's \emph{informativeness} is
\verb{|lik_sd_sens|}. (\code{lik_mean_sens} is reported but not used: for a
symmetric posterior, tempering prior and likelihood together leaves the
mean unchanged, so \code{lik_mean_sens = -prior_mean_sens}.)
\itemize{
\item \code{"-"}: the prior's influence is below \code{threshold}; the likelihood
dominates.
\item \code{"weak likelihood"}: the prior is influential and the likelihood is
not informative; the data say little about this parameter.
\item \code{"prior-data conflict"}: prior influential, likelihood informative, and
\verb{|conflict_z| >= z_threshold}; prior and data disagree.
\item \code{"informative prior"}: prior influential and likelihood informative,
but they agree; fine if the prior is justified.
}

This refines the diagnosis of Kallioinen et al. (2023), which flags
potential conflict whenever both prior and likelihood sensitivity are
high, by separating informative-but-compatible priors from conflicting
ones.

\strong{Finite perturbations.} For each value in \code{alpha}, the posterior under
the power-scaled prior (and likelihood) is approximated by Pareto-smoothed
importance sampling (Vehtari et al., 2024). The Pareto \eqn{\hat{k}}
diagnostic indicates whether the approximation is reliable. Weakening
(\code{alpha < 1}) is the harder direction: for an approximately normal
posterior the importance weights have a Pareto tail with \eqn{k \approx
1 - \alpha} or heavier, so values much below 0.8 are often unreliable.
Use \code{\link[=sensitivity_analysis]{sensitivity_analysis()}} to assess large perturbations by refitting.

\strong{Posterior contraction} is \eqn{1 - \sigma^2_{post}/\sigma^2_{prior}}
(Schad et al., 2021): values near 0 mean the data barely updated the
prior. It is \code{NA} when the prior variance is infinite (flat, Cauchy,
Student-t with \code{df <= 2}).

The intercept is analysed at the predictor means, which is the parameter
rstanarm places its prior on. Supported: models from
\code{\link[rstanarm:stan_glm]{rstanarm::stan_glm()}} with normal, Student-t, Cauchy, Laplace, flat or
exponential priors, fitted without \code{QR = TRUE}.
}
